// Practice quiz: Environment settings for the API address
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/environment-settings-for-the-api-address

::o1 prefix::Which .env line makes a value available as import.meta.env in client code by default? {
=VITE_REPORTS_URL\=http\://localhost\:9090 #Correct. Only VITE_-prefixed names are exposed by default.
~REPORTS_URL\=http\://localhost\:9090 #Not quite. Without the prefix, it stays out of client code.
~export REPORTS_URL\=http\://localhost\:9090 #Not quite. That form adds no prefix.
~PUBLIC_REPORTS_URL\=http\://localhost\:9090 #Not quite. That is not the default prefix in Vite.
}

::o1 value types::A .env file has VITE_RETRIES\=3. What is the type of import.meta.env.VITE_RETRIES? {
=string #Correct. Env values are strings, so convert them yourself.
~number #Not quite. Vite does not convert the value.
~bigint #Not quite. Vite does not convert the value.
~undefined #Not quite. It has the prefix, so it is defined.
}

::o1 mode override::Both .env and .env.production define VITE_API_URL. What does npm run build use? {
=The value from .env.production #Correct. Mode-specific files take priority.
~The value from .env #Not quite. The generic file has lower priority.
~Both, joined together #Not quite. One value wins.
~Neither, so the build fails #Not quite. Duplicates are expected.
}

::o2 public bundle::Why must a VITE_ variable never hold a secret? {
=Its value is written into the JavaScript that browsers download #Correct. Anyone can read that file.
~Vite encrypts it badly #Not quite. It is not encrypted at all.
~It is committed to Git automatically #Not quite. Git is a separate concern.
~It expires after each build #Not quite. It does not expire.
}

::o2 calling a paid API::Your page needs data from a paid service that requires a secret key. What is the right design? {
=The browser calls your API, and your server calls the paid service with the key #Correct. The key stays on the server.
~Put the key in .env.local with the VITE_ prefix #Not quite. It would still be inlined in the bundle.
~Encode the key in base64 in the client #Not quite. Encoding is easily reversed.
~Ask users to paste it #Not quite. That does not protect your key.
}
